 between Tossup and Lean Democratic, with none favoring the incumbent.", "watch": "Mackenzie's attacks on Brooks's court and debt history, plus a Green Party candidate on the ballot, could peel off just enough votes to flip the result.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Ryan Mackenzie (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Pennsylvania's 7th District sits near even on partisanship, and Republican Ryan Mackenzie won it by the narrowest margin in the state two years ago. His contest against Democrat Bob Brooks is again among the most competitive House races in the country, with expert raters split between Tossup and Lean Democratic and none calling it for the incumbent.\n\nThe national environment leans Democratic, with low Trump approval giving the party an edge in Pennsylvania congressional races. A September Franklin & Marshall survey put Brooks ahead 45-40, and prediction markets price the Democrat around 80 percent. Mackenzie counters with a large fundraising lead and the power of incumbency, while Brooks has drawn far more outside spending support but has faced unflattering headlines about a personal debt dispute. A Green Party candidate adds a layer of uncertainty to the final tally.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7234, "margin": 3.52}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7148, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7673, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7925, "margin": 4.5}]}